 "generatorWeaknesses": [
  "SKY OPEN is unusable at level 1: its adnominal form lists the grammar id L3.neun_attr in the step grammar, which has no construction in grammar.json (GrammarCoverage test fails). Level 1 and 2 speak it as 밖에서, but the id list ignores the level, so no level-1 trail can use SKY:OPEN yet.",
  "The verb-object pair limit (3) is nearly spent for the everyday level-1 objects: PLACE torch, PLACE candle, KINDLE and PLACE campfire, DRINK milk, EAT bread, CRAFT bread, CRAFT glass_pane, PLANT poppy all stand at 3 before the batch. New trails must switch to another id (oxeye_daisy instead of poppy) or another verb, which bends the story.",
  "A step that teaches several words needs an explicit hint (WaveQaTest), even when the generated hint would contain every noun. Eight hints in this batch are copies of the generated clue for that reason.",
  "Authored clues on a conditioned step must contain the condition phrase word for word (밤에, 맑을 때, 사다리를 들고). That keeps the poems natural, but the prefix is put in front when a clue says the condition in other words.",
  "Y_RANGE and a place verb with 에서 double up: '밤에 높이 90블록 위에서 침대에서 자요.' The Y_RANGE phrase ends in 에서 and so does the sleep phrase.",
  "REACH_HEIGHT below Y 100 is spoken 언덕 위로 and has no number or object, so its only handles for signature variety are conditions (HOLDING, TIME, WEATHER). Two of the three free handles are taken by BATCH-005 trails.",
  "HOLDING and PLAYER_REL anchors must exist in the world: PLAYER_REL on a carpet or a campfire works only when an earlier step (or the player) placed it. The generator cannot say that the thing is placed first.",
  "The relation-giveaway check for German also flags 'bei' ('bei Regen'), so a relation step must say 'Wenn es regnet' instead.",
  "Smelting a potato to a baked potato is spoken '감자를 구워요' with no input shown, and the learner cannot tell from the Korean that the potato is raw.",
  "A first-teach concept must appear in an authored clue as its dictionary form: the verb 올라가다 never matches 올라가요, so a climb step teaches the noun (밤, 하늘) first and the verb second."
 ]
